SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The ggeetteennvv() function conforms to ISO/IEC 9899:1990 (“ISO C90”).  The
     sseetteennvv(), ppuutteennvv() and uunnsseetteennvv() functions conforms to IEEE Std
     1003.1-2001 (“POSIX.1”).  The sseeccuurree__ggeetteennvv() function conforms to .

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The functions sseetteennvv() and uunnsseetteennvv() appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
     The ppuutteennvv() function appeared in 4.3BSD-Reno.

     Until FreeBSD 7.0, ppuutteennvv() would make a copy of _s_t_r_i_n_g and insert it
     into the environment using sseetteennvv().  This was changed to use _s_t_r_i_n_g as
     the memory location of the ``name=value'' pair to follow the POSIX speci‐
     fication.

     The cclleeaarreennvv() and sseeccuurree__ggeetteennvv() functions were added in FreeBSD 14.

BBUUGGSS
     Successive calls to sseetteennvv() that assign a larger-sized _v_a_l_u_e than any
     previous value to the same _n_a_m_e will result in a memory leak.  The
     FreeBSD semantics for this function (namely, that the contents of _v_a_l_u_e
     are copied and that old values remain accessible indefinitely) make this
     bug unavoidable.  Future versions may eliminate one or both of these se‐
     mantic guarantees in order to fix the bug.

